# Pandoc Documentation Creator - Help

## Overview

This project provides a complete Pandoc-based documentation system with Docker support. It includes:

- Professional LaTeX templates
- Code syntax highlighting with VSCode Light theme
- PlantUML and Mermaid diagram support
- Automatic table of contents generation
- Configurable metadata and styling

## Quick Start

> **Note:** Ensure the `.vscode/settings.json` for this project is located in the top-level directory you are working in (your current project root). This is required for the YAML schema settings to apply correctly.

### 1. Initialize Your Project

Run the initialization script to create the project structure:

```bash
./init.sh new
```

This creates:

- `{{DOCS_DIR}}/` - Your documentation chapters
- `media/` - Images and diagrams
- `out/` - Generated PDFs
- Default configuration files

### 2. Write Your Documentation

Edit markdown files in the `{{DOCS_DIR}}/` directory:

```bash
{{DOCS_DIR}}/
  01-introduction.md
  02-setup.md
  03-usage.md
```

### 3. Build Your PDF

```bash
docker run --rm  \
-v "$(pwd):/data" \
--entrypoint=/bin/sh \
"registry.gitlab.com/woofi4/public/pandoc-documentation-creator:${VERSION}" \
-c "/data/buildPDF"
```

Your PDF will be in `out/{{OUTPUT_NAME}}.pdf`

## Inputs & Bibliography

Builds require explicit inputs. Provide one of:

- `INPUT_FILES`: space-separated list of markdown files.
- `INPUT_GLOB`: glob like `/data/chapters/*.md`.
- `INPUT_DIR`: a directory containing `.md` files.

Example:

```bash
-e INPUT_FILES="/data/chapters/01-introduction.md /data/chapters/02-usage.md"
```

The default syntax highlighting theme is **VSCode Light**.

For citations, add a `references.bib` file and reference it in metadata or set it explicitly:

```yaml
bibliography: references.bib
```

```bash
-e BIBLIOGRAPHY="/data/references.bib"
```

Use `@key` in markdown to cite entries from your bibliography, for example:

```markdown
See @knuth1984 for details.
```

Add new entries in BibTeX format:

```bibtex
@book{knuth1984,
  title   = {The TeXbook},
  author  = {Donald E. Knuth},
  year    = {1984},
  publisher = {Addison-Wesley}
}
```

## Project Structure

```text
your-project/
├── {{DOCS_DIR}}/           # Documentation chapters (markdown)
├── media/             # Images, diagrams, assets
├── out/               # Generated PDF output
│   └── build/         # Build logs and LaTeX artifacts
├── metadata.yaml      # Document metadata (title, author, etc.)
└── buildPDF           # Build helper run inside Docker
```

## Configuration

The Pandoc defaults configuration is built into the Docker image. You only need to customize the document metadata.

### Metadata (metadata.yaml)

Configure document properties:

```yaml
title: "My Documentation"
author: "Your Name"
date: "2026"
lang: "de-AT"
documentclass: scrartcl
papersize: a4
```

All Pandoc behavior (filters, templates, styling) is pre-configured in the Docker image. You only customize the document-specific metadata.

## Features

### Code Highlighting

Supports multiple languages with syntax highlighting:

````markdown
```python
def hello():
    print("Hello, World!")
```
````

### Diagrams

#### PlantUML

```plantuml
@startuml
Alice -> Bob: Hello
Bob -> Alice: Hi!
@enduml
```

#### Mermaid

```mermaid
graph TD
    A[Start] --> B[Process]
    B --> C[End]
```

Limit generated diagram size with fenced-code attributes:

````markdown
```{.plantuml max-width=70%}
@startuml
Alice -> Bob: Hello
Bob -> Alice: Hi!
@enduml
```

```{.mermaid max-width=12cm}
graph TD
    A[Start] --> B[Process]
    B --> C[End]
```
````

Supported `max-width` values include percentages, `cm`, `mm`, `in`, `pt`, `px`, and LaTeX dimensions such as `0.7\linewidth`.

#### Ready-made diagram recipes

You can build richer visuals — colored concept cards, styled sequence diagrams,
circular process cycles — straight from Markdown, without any external tooling.
Copy-paste recipes for these are in `examples/advanced/diagram-cookbook.md`:

- **Concept cards in a row** (e.g. the 5 V's) — a Mermaid `flowchart` with nodes
  joined by invisible links (`~~~`) and colored via `classDef`.
- **Styled sequence diagram** — a Mermaid `sequenceDiagram` with activation bars,
  side notes and `rect rgb(...)` highlight regions.
- **Circular process cycle** (e.g. CRISP-DM) — a Graphviz graph with
  `layout=circo` and filled, colored nodes chained in a loop.

Pick the closest recipe, swap in your own labels and colors, and rebuild.

#### Native TikZ diagrams (neural networks, custom figures)

For node-and-edge figures such as perceptrons or multi-layer networks, you can
embed **TikZ** directly — it is compiled by LaTeX, so no external service is
involved. The common libraries (`arrows.meta`, `positioning`, `calc`, `fit`) are
preloaded, so just write the picture inside a raw LaTeX block:

````markdown
```{=latex}
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=Stealth]
  \node[circle,draw] (a) at (0,0) {$x_1$};
  \node[circle,draw] (b) at (2,0) {$\Sigma$};
  \draw[->] (a) -- (b);
\end{tikzpicture}
```
````

Wrap it in `\begin{figure}[H]...\caption{...}\end{figure}` for a numbered,
captioned figure. Ready-to-copy perceptron and multi-layer-network recipes are in
`examples/advanced/diagram-cookbook.md`. TikZ renders only in the PDF build, not
in Markdown previews.

### Images

```markdown
![Caption](media/image.png)
```

#### SVG Images (Auto-Converted)

SVG files cannot be embedded directly in LaTeX/PDF output. During the build, referenced `.svg` images are automatically rasterized using **Inkscape**:

- Default output format: PNG
- Optional output format: JPG (requires ImageMagick)

You can control this behavior via environment variables:

- `SVG_EXPORT_FORMAT`: `png` (default) or `jpg`
- `SVG_EXPORT_DPI`: export resolution (default: `300`)
- `SVG_EXPORT_JPG_QUALITY`: JPG quality (default: `92`)

